Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
now returning to a story we've covered  extensively over the past week the  ongoing military lockdown in  indian administered kashmir new delhi  revoked the autonomy of the disputed  region on monday prompting a stern  response from neighbouring pakistan  sarah bin javed joins us now live from  muzaffarabad with the  foreign minister of pakistan mahmoud  quraishi  thanks sami we are with the foreign  minister who's just arrived  in muzafrabat on the pakistani side of  kashmir  where he is going to be spending eid  with kashmiris both who live here and  those who have been displaced  from the indian side mr croatia thank  you very much for being with us  you've just returned from china as  pakistan's offensive  diplomatically continues what did you  achieve and are you satisfied that the  international community uh understands  pakistan's point of view  well we have to project our point of  view and the  decision taken by the national security  committee  which was endorsed by the cabinet and  obviously demanded by  the joint session of parliament was that  we should go to the security council  and to as a first step uh  i thought it was necessary to consult  with our  ally uh someone who stood with pakistan  consistently and is one of the p5  members that's china  so i went with the delegation  to meet with the chinese leadership  met the state councillor for minister  and  presented our point of view to him  and the chinese government and i have  come back  with satisfaction that if they  completely understand  and endorse our point of view they've  considered  the indian uh action as unilateral  uh unlawful uh and they feel  that the matter should be resolved under  the u.n charter  and uh according to the applicable  security council resolutions so they  have  endorsed our point of view they are  equally concerned  about the peace and security of the  region  this action has undermined peace and  security  of the region there are concerns of  human rights violations  on the inside of kashmir  you have seen this curfew there is a  communication  blackout journalists are not being  allowed in  so the news is not filtering out there's  absolute  confusion uncertainty one is hearing a  hearing of food shortages  one is hearing of non-availability of  medicines  i do not know tomorrow is eid which is a  very important day for muslims  whether they will lift the curfew i  demand that the  indian authorities lift a curfew  and let the muslims uh perform  their prayers and do the sacrifice that  they are supposed to do mreshi the  international response to this according  to many international observers has been  muted at best the united nation  secretary general urged  both sides to refrain from changing the  status of kashmir  the emirati ambassador went out and said  that it is an internal matter  for the indian government so is the  pakistani government running out of  options diplomatically  to try and come to some sort of a  solution not at all i think the uh  options are just beginning in fact uh  india by being obstinate  and not uh accepting pakistan's  offer of a bilateral engagement  to resolve this issue uh through  peaceful negotiations  miscalculated and i think this decision  of theirs  of revoking uh and bringing in the  change  constitutional change which has  materially affected  uh the status of uh uh  kashmir has  given an opportunity uh to pakistan in  my view  to take the case to the security council  because it is  already on the agenda it's a pending  agenda item  on the security council and we expect  and we will argue that this step has  uh there are concerns vis-a-vis peace  and security  uh in south asia and we are concerned  about the  genocide that we feel can take place  perhaps is taking place right now  because we have  no idea what's going on uh in the indian  occupied kashmir right now  when they lifted the curfew for a few  hours you saw  thousands of people protesting on the  streets  so it's an evolving situation pakistan  is watching the situation carefully  and so is the world the statement of the  secretary general of the u.n is a very  balanced and very correct statement he  has said  that this issue should be resolved under  the u.n charter and  applicable security council resolutions  i think that's a very  encouraging statement the support  offered to pakistan by china  that they are with us all the way  is very encouraging and uh prime  minister mahad khan  is reaching out to world leaders uh i'm  contacting  different foreign ministers uh for  example i spoke to the  kuwaiti foreign minister this morning uh  he was very supportive  uh very encouraging so it is building  up in many countries european countries  when i spoke to the  high representative of the european  union  uh fredericka mogherini she was  concerned  on the uh humanitarian issue  you know the the human rights violations  the reports that they are getting from  uh  from from you know from people all over  are alarming  they are alarming and they're concerned  about it but there's also concern on the  pakistani side as well i've spent the  day at the line of control talking to  ordinary kashmiris  who feel that pakistan perhaps has not  done  enough uh that is something that was  shared on the parliament's floor by the  opposition  as well where the prime minister said  that you know pakistan cannot go to war  how would you if you've just taken the  decision to come and spend either with  them  how what would you tell them which will  satisfy them that pakistan is doing all  it can  or for the kashmir to begin with why am  i here  to give a message of solidarity  i represent the government of pakistan  and i am here with the permission of the  prime minister of pakistan  i discussed it with him last night and  he said by all means you must go  and show solidarity he himself is coming  to muzaffarabad  to address the uh the assembly on the  14th of august  we have declared uh 14th of august  which is our independence day to be a  day  of uh solidarity uh with uh with the  kashmiris on both sides of the loc  and we've announced the 15th of august  is going to be a black day  observed by kashmiris and pakistanis  all over the world in every capital  there will be  a standing shoulder to shoulder  with their kashmiri brothers and sisters  what are your biggest fears and what is  the hope that you have  that pakistan's version of events  pakistan's  what pakistan's point of view is is  going to be heard and it will prevail  my biggest fear is the  the genocide that can  take place on the inside of cash which  we have no evidences yet  well open up open up  you were here did anybody stop you you  went to the loc you met people you're  interviewing people  will they allow you there vic why don't  you attempt  and try after having been here  go and make a request to the indian side  and let them allow you to go to sri  nagar  to meet the leadership over there will  they allow you i doubt it  so we have nothing to fair we have  nothing to hide  and uh i think pakistan has a solid  case and we're going to articulate that  case we're going to put the case in  front of the world community  international community and we are  concerned about  the uh violations that are taking place  because today in india  you have a regime which has the fascist  mentality  you know that nazi approach that they  have  is that rss mentality  you know this is a political wing of the  rss  and that is of great concern uh  to pakistan innocent people  being attacked killed raped  maimed and through the pellet guns you  know losing eyesight  what is going to be what is your biggest  fear is i ask you because  the world is watching closely that two  nuclear armed rivals  are now in the battle for words uh where  they  don't you've cut off well almost uh cut  off diplomatic relations you're  downgraded to the maximum level  you've cut off trade responsible for  that so  is it who is responsible for that my  question is is there  a fear that this could lead to an  escalation where  it would go to a point of no return well  we are fearing  that to divert world attention  from the atrocities that are being  committed uh  in indian occupied cashmere the indians  might undertake a false  flag operation and then turn  uh sort of a twist events and distort  uh events and say ha you know this was  a cross border terrorism you know  the pulwama ii that staged drama that  they did  in february they would try and create uh  some kind of a  situation to divert uh  world attention and that is why we are  saying  that on that pre-text they might do  something stupid  what they have done already that was a  stupid move now  it's a rash move in our view and uh  we're going to tell the world it's it's  a responsibility you know  yes you said yes there are two nuclear  armed states  uh and we think uh a war is not a  solution  uh it's mutual suicide that's never been  an option  our response even in february was  defensive in nature we were not  aggressive  so we are going to articulate  our case diplomatically  politically and we will look at the  legal options  uh we will see besides the security  council what options do we have  what are those options because people  have been talking about people have been  talking about pakistan stepping up its  military presence on the line of control  stopping air space what are those  options that you're saying incorrect  we have not we have not stopped airspace  let me qualify that let me clarify that  we have not they are in a habit of  generating  uh fake news uh and and they you know  they take advantage of that  but we have not uh we have not  uh are you going to sorry are you going  to  we haven't taken that decision yet but  it depends on  how they react and how they behave and  what is their attitude  uh what we are saying is we have a case  that case is pending the security  council they have rejected  they have rejected mediation they have  rejected third party facilitation  and they are also uh  shy of a bilateral interaction  and on top of that they are changing the  status  of jammu and kashmir which is a disputed  territory internationally recognized  dispute  so what do we do the only rational  reasonable uh option with pakistan was  go  to the security council and tell the  security council  it is their responsibility it's on their  agenda  it's a pending item and peace and  security is their prime responsibility  mr qureshi thank you very much for being  with us uh well there you have it that  was the  foreign minister of pakistan china with  kirishi speaking to us telling us that  pakistan believes  in a diplomatic solution out of this  crisis pakistan is going to  employ all of its resources uh to find  uh support for its version of events  and is going to be going to the security  council soon all right great interview  there thanks so much summer benjamin

